What in tools/russian is whose, and what goes where.

None of it reaches a build whose LANGS does not name lang/ruru: the Makefile
runs nothing here and compiles nothing of Russian's for such a build, so a
library without Russian carries none of what is listed below.

openrussian.tsv.gz is made from the dictionary of OpenRussian
(https://en.openrussian.org), as published at
https://github.com/Badestrand/russian-dictionary. It keeps, for each inflected
form of each word there, which vowel the stress falls on, and drops the rest:
translations, grammar and the forms of one syllable. openrussian.py is what
made it.

That dictionary is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike
4.0 International licence, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/,
and so is openrussian.tsv.gz, and so is the table tools/russian/stress.py
writes out of it, build/ruru/eci_russian_stress.h, and the part of any built
library that table becomes -- which is to say every library built with
lang/ruru, and only those. Anyone redistributing them has to say where they
came from, as this file does, and pass them on under the same licence.

rulex/ is RuLex, Igor B. Poretsky's pronunciation dictionary for Russian
(https://github.com/poretsky/rulex), as its source files are published, the
test dictionary gzipped. It is licensed under the GNU General Public
License, version 2; rulex/LICENSE.GPL-2 is that licence. rulex.py turns it
into rulex.dat, which the NVDA add-on carries beside the engine's library.
rulex.dat is a file the engine reads when it is there, not part of the
engine: the engine speaks Russian without it, and nothing GPL is compiled
into any library. Wherever rulex.dat goes, its complete corresponding source
goes with it, as the licence asks: tools/russian/make-ruru.py writes
build/rulex-source.zip, which holds rulex/ as it is here and rulex.py, the
program that makes rulex.dat out of it, and the add-on carries that zip and
the licence beside rulex.dat.

rules/ru_val.up and rules/ru_spr.up are not ours alone. They are written
for the module tools/russian/make-ruru.py makes out of lang/enus, and are
adapted from IBM's rules in it: ru_val.up begins with lines of eng_ret_Fv's
own and takes its numbers from lang/eses, and ru_spr.up is one of the
module's own wrappers, ZZlprp_load__insert_2pt_s_2_1_ZZstring34, with the code
it inserts changed. So they are IBM's data as lang/enus and
lang/eses are, governed by what NOTICE at the top of this repository says of
those, and the MIT licence does not reach them. The same is true of the
changes make-ruru.py makes to the copied module's rules, which are IBM's
rules edited, and of the module it writes, lang/ruru.

Everything else here -- stress.txt, the scripts, and the engine's Russian
reader in rom/ruru, including the code that reads rulex.dat -- is ours and
under the MIT licence in LICENSE at the top of this repository.
